    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d830">

                <pb facs="0952" n="940" sameAs="frus:frus1943v02/pg940"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/31288</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Welles</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Apostolic
                        Delegate at Washington</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Cicognani</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-08-08">August 8, 1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Archbishop Cicognani</hi>: I have received
                    this morning your letter of August 7<anchor n="52" xml:id="d830-f1"/> in which
                    you inform me that you have just received from His Eminence the Cardinal
                    Secretary of State a radiogram urgently requesting a definitive reply to his
                    previous message advising of the decision of the new Italian Government to
                    declare Rome an open city. You add that you will be grateful for anything <pb
                        facs="0953" n="941" xml:id="pg941"/> that I can do to expedite the securing
                    of the desired response so that you may be in a position to report such reply to
                    the Cardinal Secretary of State.</p>
                <p>As I informed Your Excellency in my letter of August 4,<anchor n="53"
                        xml:id="d830-f2"/> the message under reference was immediately submitted by
                    me to the highest authorities of the Government of the United States. The matter
                    is receiving their fullest consideration.</p>
                <p>In the meantime I am instructed by the President to state that, in accordance
                    with the accepted principles of international law and of pertinent international
                    agreements, there is nothing to prevent the Italian Government from undertaking
                    unilaterally to declare Rome an open city.</p>
                <p>With the assurances [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sumner Welles</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
